    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101">

                <pb facs="0272" n="144" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg144"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 101.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Pringle</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-11-28"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 28,
                        1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 307.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received Mr. Hall’s dispatch of
                    September 29 last, No. 427, relative to the plottings of ex-President Soto
                    against the peace of Honduras and the part taken in the same by the British
                    steamer Dorian, through the assistance of Messrs. A. D. Straus &amp; Co., of New
                    York.</p>
                <p>I have inclosed a copy of Mr. Hall’s dispatch to the Attorney-General and have
                    observed that it might be proper to instruct the district attorney at New York
                    City in regard to the alleged operations of Messrs. A. D. Straus &amp; Co., to
                    the end that in case they were infringing the neutrality laws of this Government
                    all necessary steps could be taken in the premises.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>T. F. BAYARD.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
